          "type": "turn",
          "question": "A claim came back asking for more specificity on a three-character code. Is that always a reasonable request?",
          "answer": "No. Whether a request for more detail is reasonable depends on the code. The sources I searched show that most ICD-10-CM categories are not complete at three characters, so a claim that asks for more detail is often correct.\n\n- **Most codes need more characters.** The coding structure summary says most categories are \"not valid at three characters\" and must be carried to their full length. Some categories also need a seventh character, and the placeholder X is used to hold empty positions open. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.)\n- **Some three-character codes are complete.** The hypertension summary says I10 (essential hypertension) \"is unusual in being a complete code at three characters.\" A request to add detail to I10 is one the classification cannot satisfy. (Source: *Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*.)\n- **Some codes default when detail is missing.** For type 2 diabetes (E11), the summary says a record that says only \"diabetes mellitus\" defaults to type 2. (Source: *Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*.)\n\nI can't tell from these sources which code your claim used, or what it was asking for, so I can't say whether this particular request was reasonable. If you share the code and the wording of the request, I can check what the sources say about that code. A billing or coding specialist, or the payer's written explanation, is the right place to confirm a specific claim.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
